  <div class="page-body">
    <section class="prose content-section" id="mission"><h2>Our mission</h2><p class="lead">Better experiments. Deeper understanding. More effective bioprocesses.</p><p>The KIWI Biolab combines automated experiments with process models and machine learning to identify suitable conditions for biotechnological applications. Our research spans library screening, process development, monitoring and control.</p><p>At TU Berlin’s Chair of Bioprocess Engineering, laboratory robotics and computational methods work together: experimental data informs models, and models help shape the next experiment.</p></section>
    <figure class="research-photo"><img src="{{ site.hero }}" alt="Parallel cultivation and automated pipetting in the KIWI Biolab" width="1080" height="721" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Parallel experiments in the KIWI Biolab · © KIWI Biolab</figcaption></figure>
    <section class="prose content-section" id="automation"><h2>Laboratory automation</h2><p>Liquid handling stations, bioreactors and analytical instruments form a connected experimental environment. Integrating equipment, software and data allows complex workflows to run with greater consistency and less manual intervention.</p><p>Our work includes device integration, scheduling and communication. The group has contributed to the <a href="https://sila-standard.com/">SiLA standard</a>, helping instruments and applications work together.</p></section>
    <section class="prose content-section" id="modeling"><h2>Model-based engineering</h2><p>Mechanistic and hybrid models describe how a bioprocess develops over time. We use them to estimate states and parameters, interpret measurements and guide experimental design.</p><p>Model predictive control connects these predictions to process decisions. It supports adaptive feeding strategies and the online redesign of experiments as new measurements become available.</p></section>
    <section class="prose content-section" id="machine-learning"><h2>Machine learning</h2><p>Data-driven methods complement biological knowledge. Research within KIWI-biolab investigates forecasting, active learning, signal processing and the combination of machine learning with dynamical process models.</p><p>The aim is to extract more knowledge from each experiment and use that knowledge to make subsequent experiments more informative.</p><p class="source-note">Research overview: <a href="{{ site.tuSource }}">KIWI Biolab at TU Berlin</a>.</p></section>
